# Intelligence Provider Registry and Scene Routing

Nexus uses one server-owned configuration model for AI, translation, OCR, image composition, and speech providers.

## Source of truth

- `provider_registry`: aggregation channels, explicit adapter format, scope, endpoint, safe metadata, and `authRef`.
- `provider_capabilities`: capabilities declared by each channel.
- `provider_secure_store`: encrypted credentials referenced by `authRef`.
- `scene_registry`: product scenes and their required capabilities.
- `scene_strategy_bindings`: ordered channel/model bindings and fallback order for each scene capability.

The retired `intelligence_providers` table, provider mirror bridge, dashboard provider CRUD, migration endpoint, and CoreApp provider-sync endpoint are not runtime configuration sources.

## Runtime contract

Clients send a capability ID and input. They do not select a provider, endpoint, adapter, credential, price, or model. Nexus resolves the canonical scene, then selects its ordered binding:

```text
capability request
  -> canonical scene
  -> binding (provider + adapter + model + priority)
  -> protected credential
  -> provider adapter
  -> credit settlement + safe usage trace
```

Fallback is sequential. Nexus tries the next binding only after a definitive pre-acceptance rejection. An accepted, uncertain, cancelled-after-send, or partially streamed request is never replayed through another upstream.

## Configuration rules

- `metadata.adapterKey` is mandatory when a provider declares capabilities and must name a registered adapter.
- `metadata.defaultModel`, when present, must be included in `metadata.models`.
- A scene binding may select a model only from its provider model list.
- System providers are available to authenticated users and remain credit-gated. User providers are owner-only; unresolved workspace scope fails closed.
- Missing provider/capability/adapter/model bindings project the scene as `degraded`; an incomplete scene must not appear healthy.
- Credentials, prompts, media, transcripts, upstream response bodies, and native errors never enter ordinary provider metadata or usage ledgers.

## Speech

Nexus-owned buffered `audio.stt` and uploaded `audio.transcribe` use the same Scene + Provider Registry selection. Direct realtime voice transports remain CoreApp-owned. Nexus speech services retain media validation, idempotency, accepted/uncertain dispatch fencing, transcript privacy, and durable credit settlement.

## Legacy table removal

Application startup never runs destructive DDL. Operators use the guarded `provider:legacy:drop` command only after it:

1. exports a D1 backup;
2. confirms enabled registry providers and scenes exist;
3. confirms every enabled credentialed provider has a secure-store row;
4. receives both `--execute` and the explicit destructive acknowledgement environment variable.

Audits, IP bans, provider health, usage ledgers, and credit records are independent of the retired provider table and remain available. The prompt registry, prompt bindings and runtime session/trace/checkpoint tables belonged to the admin agent workspace retired on 2026-10-02: their rows stay in D1 untouched, but no code reads or writes them any more.
